Case 2:
The issue with the update query is that it tries to select from the same table being updated, which is not allowed in MySQL. The proposed solution uses UPDATE IGNORE to avoid duplicate conflicts and suggests reading about INSERT ON DUPLICATE KEY for a similar approach.

Case 3:
The two queries are different because of the way MINUS works. MINUS removes duplicates based on all columns specified in the SELECT clause, whereas NOT IN only checks if the value exists in the subquery. In this case, the first query returns fewer records because it eliminates duplicate rows, while the second query returns more records because it does not eliminate duplicates.

